# {{.Name}}

A service built on the [rizotto]({{.RizottoModule}}) framework, generated by `rizotto make-project`.
The go module follows the git repository: `{{.Repo}}`.

## Getting started

```sh
task deps        # resolve go dependencies
{{- if .SQL}}
task migrate-up  # apply the migrations to $DATABASE_URL
{{- end}}
task start       # run the service on $PORT (9090 by default)
task test        # run the tests
task lint        # run golangci-lint
task gen         # regenerate the service bindings{{if .SQL}} and the sqlc queries{{end}}
```

Configuration is read from `.env`, which imports the defaults from `.env.example`.
Values already present in the environment win over both files.

If `task deps` cannot download `{{.RizottoModule}}`, either export
`GOPRIVATE={{.RizottoModule}}` or point the module at a local checkout:

```sh
go mod edit -replace {{.RizottoModule}}=../rizotto
```

## How it works

```sh
rizotto skill              # what this project is made of
rizotto service skill      # services in detail
rizotto controller skill   # controllers in detail
```

`.claude/skills/rizotto/SKILL.md` points Claude Code at those same commands, so an
agent working in this repository asks the installed binary instead of guessing at
the conventions.

## Layout

    server.go                     wires the settings, the services and the HTTP routes
    .claude/skills/rizotto/       the Claude Code skill describing how to work in this project
    api/controller.go             HTTP contexts shared by the controllers (authentication lives here)
    api/doc/                      the OpenAPI document the route tests fill in
    services/item/                the item service: the RPC implementation
    services/item/item-client/    the item contract; bind-gen.go is generated from client.go by genbind
{{- if .SQL}}
    services/item/repository/     database access: sqlc queries, generated db package and dbmate migrations
{{- end}}

The sample `item` service is a starting point: rename it, or add the next service
with the command below.

## Adding a service

```sh
rizotto service add
```

It asks for the service name, whether it works with the database and which CRUD
methods it needs, writes `services/<name>/`{{if .SQL}}, registers the queries in `sqlc.yaml`{{end}}
and prints the lines to add to `server.go`. Afterwards run `task gen` to regenerate
`bind-gen.go` (it needs the `genbind` binary:
`go install {{.RizottoModule}}/tools/genbind@latest`){{if .SQL}} and `task migrate-up` to create the table{{end}}.

## Adding an HTTP controller

```sh
rizotto controller add
```

It asks which service the controller exposes, which routes it serves, whether they
need an authenticated user and under which prefix they live, then writes
`api/<name>-api/` with the controller and its route tests and prints the route table
to join in `server.go`. The controller builds its context with `PrivateArea` or
`PublicArea` from `api/controller.go`; `task test` runs the route tests, which write
`api/<name>-api/openapi.yml` from the document in `api/doc`.
{{- if .SQL}}

## Database

`sqlc.yaml` generates `services/<name>/repository/db` from
`services/<name>/repository/sql`. After changing a query or the schema run
`task gen` (it uses Docker). Migrations live in
`services/<name>/repository/migrations`; `task migrate-up` applies the migrations of
every service with [dbmate](https://github.com/amacneil/dbmate).
{{- end}}
